<section class="hero"><div class="wrap hero-in"><div>
<p class="kick">Movement &middot; Fit &middot; Care</p>
<h1>A watch is a machine. Treat it like one.</h1>
<p class="lead">WristCo is an independent watch site. Three free tools and two long guides on case proportions, accuracy you can actually measure, and what a water resistance rating really permits.</p>
<p class="hero-cta"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the tools</a> <a class="btn gh" href="watch-anatomy-guide.html">How watches work</a></p></div>
<figure class="hero-fig"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777457900943-7fd2f7c42529?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="A classic silver watch with a black leather strap" width="800" height="800" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></section>
<div class="wrap"><div class="specs">
<div><b>3</b><span>free tools</span></div><div><b>6</b><span>water ratings decoded</span></div><div><b>0</b><span>affiliate links</span></div><div><b>None</b><span>sponsored reviews</span></div></div></div>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">The basics</p><h2>Five things that matter</h2></div><a href="watch-anatomy-guide.html">Full guide &rarr;</a></div>
<div class="rows">
<div class="row"><h3>01 / Movement</h3><p>Quartz keeps time to seconds a month and needs a battery. Mechanical movements keep time to seconds a day, run on a wound spring and can be serviced indefinitely. Everything else about a watch follows from this choice.</p></div>
<div class="row"><h3>02 / Proportion</h3><p>Case diameter is only half the story. Thickness, lug-to-lug length and how far the lugs curve down decide whether a watch sits flat on your wrist or rocks on it.</p></div>
<div class="row"><h3>03 / Accuracy</h3><p>A steady gain or loss is easy to live with. An unpredictable rate is not. Measure over a week before deciding anything is wrong, because position changes the result.</p></div>
<div class="row"><h3>04 / Water resistance</h3><p>The number on the back is a static pressure rating, not a swimming depth. It also decays as gaskets age, which is why pressure testing exists.</p></div>
<div class="row"><h3>05 / Service</h3><p>Oils thicken and seals harden whether or not the watch is worn. A service every five to seven years costs far less than the repair that replaces it.</p></div>
</div></div></section>
<section class="tools" id="tools"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">Free tools</p><h2>Measure, do not guess</h2></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="strap"><h3>Strap Size Finder</h3><p>Wrist measurement plus case width gives you proportion, likely lug width and strap lengths.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="sWrist">Wrist circumference (mm)</label><input id="sWrist" type="number" min="120" max="230" value="175"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="sCase">Case width (mm)</label><input id="sCase" type="number" min="24" max="50" value="38"></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="sType">Strap material</label><select id="sType"><option value="leather">Leather</option><option value="steel">Steel bracelet</option><option value="fabric">Fabric / pass-through</option><option value="rubber">Rubber</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792355682520-82afdeb59257?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="A person wearing a watch with a blue strap" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;border-radius:3px"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="sStrap"></div></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="acc"><h3>Accuracy Checker</h3><p>Set it against a reliable clock, wait a few days, then see where your watch sits against its own specification.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="aDays">Days since you set it</label><input id="aDays" type="number" min="1" max="120" value="7"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="aSec">Seconds gained (+) or lost (&minus;)</label><input id="aSec" type="number" min="-600" max="600" value="35"></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="aKind">Movement type</label><select id="aKind"><option value="mech">Mechanical / automatic</option><option value="chrono">Chronometer certified</option><option value="quartz">Quartz</option><option value="hiq">High-accuracy quartz</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758815105440-bda626bff35a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="Close-up of a green watch face" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;border-radius:3px"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="aOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="water"><h3>Water Resistance Decoder</h3><p>What the number on your case back actually permits, and how much the age of the seals changes it.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fld"><label for="wRate">Printed rating</label><select id="wRate"><option value="none">No rating printed</option><option value="30">3 ATM / 30 m</option><option value="50">5 ATM / 50 m</option><option value="100" selected>10 ATM / 100 m</option><option value="200">20 ATM / 200 m</option><option value="300">30 ATM / 300 m</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="wAge">When were the seals last tested?</label><select id="wAge"><option value="new">Within the last year</option><option value="mid" selected>One to three years ago</option><option value="old">Longer ago, or never</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1774299398828-8dc74e92e53c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="A black watch resting on gravel and rocks" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;border-radius:3px"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="wOut"></div></div></div>
</div></section>
<section class="band"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1783834402995-7cb2ec3362dd?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=75" alt="" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
<div class="wrap"><p class="kick">Worth repeating</p><h2>The number on the back is a laboratory figure</h2>
<p>Water resistance is tested statically, on a new watch, with fresh gaskets. Your watch is older than that, the seals have hardened, and a hot shower stresses them more than a swimming pool does. Pressure testing is the only way to know where you stand.</p>
<p style="margin-top:1.3em"><a class="btn" href="watch-care-guide.html">Read the care guide</a></p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">Long reads</p><h2>Two guides</h2></div></div>
<div class="cards">
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1772852238970-f560aa79a512?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="A sports watch with an orange strap" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide one</p><h3>How Watches Work</h3><p>Quartz against mechanical, what an escapement does, power reserve, complications that earn their place, and how case dimensions decide fit.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="watch-anatomy-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1763355709931-77776a864a70?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="An orange-faced watch with a striped fabric strap" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide two</p><h3>Care &amp; Service Guide</h3><p>Winding and setting without damage, magnetism, strap changes, storage, when a service is due and what a watchmaker actually does.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="watch-care-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">Questions</p><h2>Asked constantly</h2></div></div>
<div class="faq"><details><summary>What does 50 m water resistance actually allow?</summary><p>Less than it sounds. The figure comes from a static pressure test, not from swimming. At 50 m a watch handles rain and hand-washing; at 100 m it is genuinely swim-safe; diving needs 200 m and a screw-down crown. Hot showers are harder on gaskets than depth is.</p></details><details><summary>Why does my mechanical watch gain time?</summary><p>Mechanical movements are measured in seconds per day, not per month, and anything from minus ten to plus twenty is normal for a good one. Position matters too: the same watch runs at different rates dial up, dial down and crown up. A sudden change usually means magnetism.</p></details><details><summary>How big a watch suits my wrist?</summary><p>Case width of roughly 18 to 22 per cent of your wrist circumference looks classic to most eyes. Thickness matters as much as diameter: anything over about 13 mm catches on a shirt cuff however neat the face looks.</p></details><details><summary>How often does a mechanical watch need servicing?</summary><p>Typically every five to seven years, or when it starts losing power reserve or drifting badly. Quartz watches need a service far less often but should have seals replaced whenever the back is opened for a battery.</p></details><details><summary>Is an automatic better than a quartz watch?</summary><p>Neither is better; they do different things. Quartz keeps far better time and needs almost nothing. Mechanical movements are serviceable, last indefinitely with care and are the reason people enjoy watches at all. Pick for how you want to live with it.</p></details></div></div></section>
<section class="nl"><div class="wrap"><div><p class="kick">Newsletter</p><h2>The WristCo Bulletin</h2>
<p>One short email a month: a movement explained, a care mistake worth avoiding, and anything new in the guides. No spam, unsubscribe any time.</p></div>
